# The production environment, shared half — a template.
#
# The real file lives on the VPS at $HOME/proxiapay/.env.production, is chmod 600,
# and is never committed. Copy this there and fill in every <…>. The owner half —
# database owner credentials and everything the backups need — is
# deploy/production.owner.env.example, which becomes .env.production.owner.
#
# WHAT READS WHICH FILE
#   .env.production        api, worker and migrate (env_file), and Compose's own
#                          ${…} interpolation (--env-file). THIS FILE.
#   .env.production.owner  db, migrate and backup only. Named by OWNER_ENV_FILE
#                          below. api and worker never see it, so they never hold
#                          the database owner's password or the backup keys.
# So this file must NOT hold POSTGRES_PASSWORD, the owner DATABASE_URL,
# APP_DB_ROLE, APP_DB_PASSWORD or any WALG_* / AWS_* / BACKUP_* variable. A test
# (apps/api/src/docs/production-env.test.ts) enforces it.
#
# Values go in literally. Compose interpolates `$` in env files, so generate
# secrets with `openssl rand -hex` (letters and digits only), which cannot hold one.
#
# It is named `production.env.example` rather than `.env.production.example` only
# because a dot-env name is unwritable under this machine's tooling guard.

# ── What Compose itself reads ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# The services declare `env_file: ${ENV_FILE:-.env.production}`; set it anyway so
# the intent is on the page.
ENV_FILE=.env.production

# The owner half (see above). Without it the db would start from this file, find
# no POSTGRES_PASSWORD and refuse to initialise.
OWNER_ENV_FILE=.env.production.owner

# Keeps a manual `docker compose --env-file .env.production …` on the production
# override too (backups and WAL archiving live in it). Without it a hand-run `up`
# would recreate db from the plain image and stop archiving. The deploy script
# names both files itself.
COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.deploy.yml:docker-compose.production.yml

# Separates the Compose project, the container names and the Traefik routers from
# every other stack on this VPS, the sandbox included. The deploy REFUSES to run
# unless Compose reports the project `proxiapay`.
STACK=proxiapay

# One hostname serves everything: Traefik sends /v1, /console, /providers and /docs
# to the api, and everything else to the console.
APP_DOMAIN=pay.proxia-digital.com

# `le` validates by TLS-ALPN-01, which requires the name to reach this VPS
# directly. It does: pay.proxia-digital.com is a plain A record to the VPS.
# THE DAY THIS NAME GOES BEHIND THE ORANGE CLOUD, switch to `le-dns`, or the
# renewal fails silently sixty days later.
CERT_RESOLVER=le

REGISTRY=ghcr.io/iamvaln
# IMAGE_TAG is deliberately NOT here. A deploy sets it from the release tag, and a value stored
# in this file goes stale at the next release, so a hand-run command would use old images.
# Compose stops with "set IMAGE_TAG" if it is unset; the `dc` alias in
# docs/production-deployment.md derives it from the tag the checkout is on.

# ── What the platform reads ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `production` selects the real provider account at seed time, enforces declared
# origins, and makes the api and worker refuse to start as a database superuser
# or as a role able to UPDATE any table that refuses mutation (ledger_entry among them).
PROXIAPAY_ENV=production
NODE_ENV=production
PORT=3000
LOG_LEVEL=info

# Exactly one proxy, Traefik, stands in front of the api. docker-compose.deploy.yml
# sets the same value; it is here for the record and for the config check.
TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1

# api and worker connect as the least-privilege role migrate creates and
# re-grants. Its password is APP_DB_PASSWORD in the owner file: the two must
# agree. Host `db`, database as POSTGRES_DB in the owner file.
# LEAVE THIS OUT and DATABASE_URL (the owner's URL is in the other file) reaches api and worker
# empty: they stop on a configuration error. They never fall back to the owner. And never
# `export` DATABASE_URL or APP_DATABASE_URL in the deploy user's shell: an exported variable
# overrides --env-file.
APP_DATABASE_URL=postgres://proxiapay_app:<the same as APP_DB_PASSWORD>@db:5432/proxiapay

# THESE TWO ARE A DOCUMENTED DEVIATION from docs/operations.md, which wants them
# injected from a key store. This VPS has none, so they live here, mode 600 (see
# docs/production-deployment.md, "The documented deviation"). FRESH values:
# never the sandbox's. Generate each with: openssl rand -base64 32
# LOSE EITHER and the sealed provider credentials, signing secrets, authenticator
# seeds and payer identifiers are unreadable for good. Copy both offline before
# the first real transaction.
MASTER_KEY_BASE64=<generate>
INDEX_KEY_BASE64=<generate>

# Same origin as the console, used for the cookie attributes and CORS; also the
# base the platform advertises for itself.
CONSOLE_ORIGIN=https://pay.proxia-digital.com
PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://pay.proxia-digital.com

# Mail goes through Resend. MAIL_FROM must sit on a domain verified there;
# proxia-digital.com is.
RESEND_API_KEY=<the Resend key>
MAIL_FROM=ProxiaPay <pay@proxia-digital.com>

# Where alerts land. The three group addresses are seeded once into each group's
# delivery addresses; the console owns them afterwards. A group without one falls
# back to ALERT_EMAIL.
ALERT_EMAIL=<your address>
ALERT_EMAIL_FINANCE=
ALERT_EMAIL_DEVELOPERS=
ALERT_EMAIL_ADMINISTRATORS=

# There is deliberately NO second-factor switch in this file. The console's
# second factor is the real authenticator in production, and the api refuses to
# start if the sandbox's any-six-digits switch is set.
